%erg 0.1
Title: erg new slug truncation can trigger the pathIsClosed component test
Created: 2026-07-02
Author: MinhHaDuong

--- log ---
2026-07-02T07:49Z MinhHaDuong created
2026-09-16T07:45Z MinhHaDuong note Author set the basename check at error level per the spec's hard-violation rule, not warn. Side effect: makes erg-github's basename-blind ticket_is_closed() unreachable in a well-formed store.

--- body ---
## Problem

`erg new` kebab-cases the title and truncates the slug to 40 characters, but never checks the result against the `pathIsClosed` component test (`closed`, `closed-*`, `closed.*`, `*-closed`). A title that mentions "closed" mid-sentence can therefore produce a file that the whole toolchain silently treats as a closed ticket.

Observed live on 2026-07-02: `erg new "erg-pr-merge regex misses tickets/closed/NNNN paths"` created `0255-erg-pr-merge-regex-misses-tickets-closed.erg` -- the 40-char truncation landed exactly on `-closed`. The ticket vanished from `erg list` and `erg ready` immediately after creation, with no warning from `new`, `validate`, or `check`.

Note the asymmetry: rule 14 rejects a *Title* that starts or ends with a status word, but nothing guards the *slug*, and truncation can manufacture a `-closed` suffix from a perfectly valid title.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] `erg new` never produces a filename for which `pathIsClosed` fires (e.g. trim or extend the truncation point, or drop the offending trailing segment)
- [ ] Regression test covering a title whose 40-char truncation lands on `-closed`
- [ ] `erg check` reports a corpus **error** (exit 1, not a warning) when an open-by-header ticket has a `pathIsClosed`-positive basename, so hand-created files get caught too

## Severity decision (author, 2026-09-16)

The basename check lands at **error** level, not warn. The Imagine pass
recommended warn, to match `check.go`'s existing severity split; the author
chose error, and the spec is why. `spec-erg-v1.md` section "Closed /
not-closed criterion" makes the path test disjunctive and names
`0001-foo-closed.erg` as a covered case, then states that placement and
`Closed:` header disagreeing is a **hard violation, exit 1**. A top-level
`-closed` basename with no header is exactly that disagreement;
`folderClosure` (`check.go:17`) simply never looks at the basename, so the
rule the spec already states goes unenforced.

This also closes a second gap for free. `ticket_is_closed()` in
`tickets/erg-github` (L46-58) applies only the directory glob and the header
test, never the basename test -- so a ticket closed by basename alone reads
as *still open* to the CI merge gate and would fail a PR that should pass.
At error level, that state becomes invalid corpus rather than something the
gate must handle, so the erg-github gap is unreachable in a well-formed
store and needs no separate ticket. At warn level it would have needed one.

## Imagine

**Premise check: LIVE.** `slugify` (`src/go/new.go:18-30`) truncates to 40 chars
at line 22-24 with a bare `strings.TrimRight(s, "-")` and no `pathIsClosed`
call anywhere in `new.go`. `TestSlugify` (`src/go/new_test.go:10-32`) pins the
40-char boundary (two cases at exactly 40/41 `a`s) but has no case landing on
a `-closed` suffix, so exit criterion 2 (regression test) is unmet in the
current tree. Separately, `check.go`'s `folderClosure` (`check.go:17-34`,
called from `validateCorpus` at `validate.go:120`, itself invoked from
`cmdCheck`, so directory/header mismatches are hard VIOLATIONs, not
warnings) only calls `pathIsClosed(filepath.Dir(t.Path))` (`check.go:21`) --
directory only, never the basename -- so a hand-crafted or
truncation-produced `NNNN-*-closed.erg`
with no `Closed:` header passes `erg check` silently today: `t.IsClosed()`
(`erg.go:36`) already returns true via the basename branch of `pathIsClosed`,
but nothing in `check.go` surfaces the mismatch as a warning. Exit criterion 3
is confirmed unmet.

**Why now / why this scope.** This is a live, previously-observed data-loss
bug (0255 vanished from `erg list`/`erg ready` with zero warning) with a
minimal, well-understood blast radius (one function in `new.go`, one warning
function in `check.go`, two test files). Fits comfortably under the
"verified empirical need" bar (STATE.md) and nowhere near monster-ticket
territory (2 files touched, not 15+).

**Simplest fix path for (a).** Because the generated basename is always
`NNNN-slug` (digits first), the only branch of `pathIsClosed` a generated slug
can ever trigger is the trailing `strings.HasSuffix(lower, "-closed")` check --
the `closed`-equality and `closed-`/`closed.`-prefix branches require the
component to *start* with "closed", which the numeric ID prefix forecloses.
So the fix does not need a generic retry loop: after the existing 40-char
truncate + trim, build the candidate basename (`id-placeholder + "-" + s`,
or just test `s` for a trailing `-closed`) and, if it would land
`pathIsClosed`-positive, strip the trailing `-closed` segment (not just the
last char) and re-run `TrimRight(s, "-")`; fall back to `"untitled"` if that
empties the slug. This is "drop the offending trailing segment" from the exit
criteria, and it is strictly simpler than "extend the truncation point"
(which would require re-scanning forward for the next word boundary and could
still land on another closed-triggering word). A fully generic guard (loop:
truncate, test full pathIsClosed, strip trailing hyphen-segment, retry until
clean) is more robust to future pathIsClosed rule changes and is barely more
code -- worth considering over the targeted suffix-only fix, but either
satisfies exit criterion 1.

**Simplest fix path for (b).** Add a basename-only warning next to (not
replacing) `folderClosure`'s directory check: for each ticket where
`pathIsClosed(t.Path)` is true purely because of the basename (i.e. the
directory-only test is false) and `t.Closed == ""`, emit a WARN (not a hard
error, to match the existing WARN-vs-VIOLATION split in `check.go` for
non-fatal corpus smells) pointing at the specific filename and telling the
author to rename it or add `Closed:`. This reuses the existing `pathIsClosed`
predicate with no new parsing logic.

**Antipattern scan: no violation.** No stdlib or vendored kebab-case/slug
utility exists to reuse -- Go's stdlib has no slugify equivalent, and
`go.mod` (`src/go/go.mod`) declares zero dependencies, consistent with this
project's standalone-binary philosophy (STATE.md: "traveling binary").
Hand-rolling ~10 lines of regex-based normalization is the appropriate scope
here, not premature abstraction; pulling in an external slug library would be
the actual YAGNI violation.

**Scope recommendation on (a) vs (b): keep together, but (a) is the load-bearing
fix and (b) is a lower-urgency complement.** They share one root cause
(nothing validates a basename against `pathIsClosed` before/after it exists)
and touch a combined 2 source files + 2 test files -- well under the
decomposition threshold. (a) alone closes the originally observed live bug (a
tool-generated file silently vanishing); (b) is genuinely independent value
(it also catches hand-created or externally-renamed files that (a) can never
see, since (a) only guards the `erg new` code path) but is a warning, not a
correctness fix for `erg new` itself. Recommendation: implement both in this
ticket as originally scoped -- the combined diff is small enough that
splitting would add ticket-admin overhead disproportionate to the work. If
executor time is constrained, (a) is the part that must not slip; (b) is the
part that could safely become a fast-follow without leaving the original
defect unfixed.

## Plan

**Premise reconfirmed.** `nextID` always yields a 4-digit numeric prefix
(`erg new`'s `filename := fmt.Sprintf("%s-%s.erg", id, slug)`, `new.go:159`),
so a generated basename component is always `digits-slug`. `pathIsClosed`
(`erg.go:46-67`) needs a component to *start* with `closed` for the
equality/prefix branches; a digit prefix forecloses both. Only
`strings.HasSuffix(lower, "-closed")` is reachable from `erg new`. Confirmed
live, not assumed.

### Actions

**(a) `src/go/new.go`**

1. Replace the body of `slugify` (`new.go:18-30`) to call a new helper after
   the existing truncate+trim, before the `s == ""` fallback:

   ```go
   func slugify(title string) string {
       s := strings.ToLower(title)
       s = nonAlphaNum.ReplaceAllString(s, "-")
       s = strings.Trim(s, "-")
       if len(s) > 40 {
           s = s[:40]
           s = strings.TrimRight(s, "-")
       }
       s = stripClosedSuffix(s)
       if s == "" {
           s = "untitled"
       }
       return s
   }

   // stripClosedSuffix repeatedly drops the trailing hyphen-segment of s as
   // long as a generated "NNNN-"+s basename would trip pathIsClosed's path
   // component test (spec-erg-v1.md "Closed / not-closed criterion"). Only
   // the trailing "-closed" suffix branch is reachable from a generated
   // slug -- the numeric ID prefix forecloses the equality and
   // "closed-"/"closed." prefix branches (ticket 0256) -- but this calls
   // the real predicate rather than hardcoding the suffix string, so it
   // stays correct if pathIsClosed's rule set ever changes.
   func stripClosedSuffix(s string) string {
       for s != "" && pathIsClosed("0000-"+s+".erg") {
           idx := strings.LastIndex(s, "-")
           if idx < 0 {
               return ""
           }
           s = strings.TrimRight(s[:idx], "-")
       }
       return s
   }
   ```

   Placed in `new.go` (not `erg.go`) since it is `erg new`-specific
   slug-shaping, not a general path predicate.

**(b) `src/go/check.go`**

2. Extend `folderClosure` (`check.go:17-34`) with a third check inside the
   existing per-ticket loop, after the two existing `if` blocks:

   ```go
   // Basename-only closure (ticket 0256): a ticket whose basename alone
   // trips pathIsClosed (hand-crafted or truncation-produced
   // NNNN-*-closed.erg) but carries no Closed: header disagrees exactly
   // the way the placement/header check above forbids -- same class of
   // hard violation, at error level (spec-erg-v1.md "Closed / not-closed
   // criterion"; author decision 2026-09-16, ticket 0256 Severity
   // decision). inClosedDir already covers every directory-closed ticket
   // (any depth, since pathIsClosed walks all components of
   // filepath.Dir(t.Path)), so basenameClosed can only be true when
   // pathIsClosed(t.Path) fires SOLELY because of the basename.
   basenameClosed := !inClosedDir && pathIsClosed(t.Path)
   if basenameClosed && !hasClosed {
       errs = append(errs, fmt.Sprintf(
           "%s: filename matches the closed-ticket pattern (spec 'Closed / not-closed criterion') but has no Closed: header -- rename the file or add 'Closed: <reason>'",
           t.Filename()))
   }
   ```

   `basenameClosed && hasClosed` needs no new branch: it is already caught
   by the existing `!inClosedDir && hasClosed` error two lines above
   ("closed ticket not in closed/ directory"), so no double-reporting.
   `folderClosure`'s return already feeds `errors` (not `corpusWarnings`)
   via `validateCorpus` (`validate.go:120`), so this lands at error/exit-1
   automatically -- no change needed to `validate.go` or `cmdCheck`.

3. Optional doc hygiene (non-blocking): extend the "Folder/header closure"
   bullet in `helpCheck` (`check.go:193-195`) to mention the basename-only
   case explicitly, so `erg check --help` matches the new behavior.

### First test (red step)

**(a) `src/go/new_test.go`**

- Add to `TestSlugify`'s table (the historical live case from this ticket's
  Problem section):
  `{"erg-pr-merge regex misses tickets/closed/NNNN paths", "erg-pr-merge-regex-misses-tickets"}`.
  Today: `slugify` returns `"erg-pr-merge-regex-misses-tickets-closed"` (40
  chars, unmodified) -- test fails, red.
- Add `TestSlugifyNeverProducesClosedBasename(t *testing.T)`: a table of
  adversarial titles including (i) the historical case above and (ii) a
  constructed exact-boundary case, `strings.Repeat("x", 32) + "-closed-extra-words-here-padding-more"`
  (kebab-cases to itself; 40-char truncation lands on `x*32 + "-closed"`
  after `TrimRight`, mirroring the original 0255 mechanism exactly rather
  than pinning one hardcoded string). For each title, assert
  `!pathIsClosed("0000-" + slugify(title) + ".erg")`. Today: both cases fail
  (pathIsClosed is true), red.

**(b) `src/go/erg_test.go`** (in `TestFolderClosure`, alongside the existing
subtests, using the `writeErg` helper already in that file)

- `"basename-only closed ticket without header errors"`: top-level file
  `0001-work-closed.erg`, headers with no `Closed:` line. Assert
  `folderClosure` returns a non-empty slice whose message contains
  `"closed-ticket pattern"`. Today: `folderClosure` returns `nil` -- red.
- `"basename-only closed ticket WITH header does not double-report"`:
  top-level file `0001-work-closed.erg` WITH `Closed: done`. Assert exactly
  one error and that it is the existing "closed ticket not in closed/
  directory" message, not two errors. (Passes today only by accident,
  since neither branch exists yet for the new message -- becomes a real
  assertion once (b) lands.)

### Dependencies

None. (a) and (b) touch disjoint files (`new.go`+`new_test.go` vs.
`check.go`+`erg_test.go`) and share no state; either can be implemented and
tested independently, in either order, inside this one ticket.

### Antipattern scan -- tautological tests

- **(a):** Would the test pass against a `slugify` that just truncates to 39
  chars instead of guarding? No. Traced by hand: the historical case's
  40-char slug is `...-closed` with the final `d` at position 40; cutting at
  39 drops only that `d`, producing `...-close` (fails to end in
  `-closed`, so it would *accidentally* look fixed) -- but the constructed
  boundary case is built precisely to defeat that accident: `x*32 +
  "-closed"` is only 39 characters long, so a 39-char cut still keeps the
  whole `-closed` suffix intact. A truncate-to-39 impl still fails
  `TestSlugifyNeverProducesClosedBasename`. Not tautological.
  Additionally, asserting through `pathIsClosed` itself (not a literal
  `strings.HasSuffix(s, "-closed")` string check in the test) means the
  test verifies the real semantic property, not an implementation detail --
  it would also catch a future regression if `pathIsClosed`'s rule set
  changes.
- **(b):** Would the test pass against a check that flags EVERY basename
  containing "closed" (`disclosed`, `enclosed` included)? Need a negative
  control to prove it does not: add
  `"disclosed/enclosed basenames do not trigger the pattern check"` --
  top-level files `0001-not-disclosed.erg` and `0002-fully-enclosed.erg`,
  no `Closed:` header. Assert `folderClosure` returns `nil`. This passes
  today (no code exists) and MUST still pass after (b) lands, because the
  implementation reuses `pathIsClosed` verbatim rather than a substring
  match -- `pathIsClosed` requires a hyphen-delimited `closed` component
  (equality, `closed-`/`closed.` prefix, or `-closed` suffix), and
  `disclosed`/`enclosed` satisfy none of those (spec-erg-v1.md line 162:
  "Rules out `disclosed`, `enclosed`"). This is the discriminating test
  the antipattern scan asks for -- without it, a naive `strings.Contains(s,
  "closed")` reimplementation would pass the positive test above and go
  undetected.

### Corpus-safety check

Ran `/tmp/erg check tickets/` (built from this worktree's `src/go`) against
the live corpus:

```
ERG CHECK: PASS (268 tickets)
```

Zero errors, zero warnings today. Scanned for existing basename-positive
files with `find tickets -iname "*-closed.erg" -o -iname "closed.erg" -o
-iname "closed-*.erg"`:

- **Top-level (outside `closed/`): zero matches anywhere in the tree.**
  Nothing in the current corpus would newly trip the (b) rule.
- **Inside `tickets/closed/` (256 archived tickets): exactly one basename
  match**, `0109-migrate-merge-archive-into-closed.erg`. Traced by hand:
  its `t.Path` is `tickets/closed/0109-migrate-merge-archive-into-closed.erg`;
  `filepath.Dir(t.Path)` is `tickets/closed`, whose `closed` component
  trips `pathIsClosed` by *equality* regardless of the basename, so
  `inClosedDir == true`. The new guard is `basenameClosed := !inClosedDir
  && pathIsClosed(t.Path)` -- the `!inClosedDir` term is `false` here, so
  `basenameClosed` is `false` unconditionally. This holds for every ticket
  under `closed/` at any depth, not just this one: `pathIsClosed` walks
  *every* path component of `filepath.Dir(t.Path)`, so any ancestor
  directory literally named (or `closed-`/`closed.`/`-closed`-matching)
  `closed` forces `inClosedDir = true` and the new branch off, independent
  of what the basename itself looks like. **Directory-closed and
  basename-closed are distinguished by construction (the `!inClosedDir`
  guard), not by inspecting the basename differently** -- so switching (b)
  to ERROR introduces zero new failures against the current 268-ticket
  corpus, confirmed by both the full `erg check` PASS and the direct
  `find` scan.
